 ### Specificity

 If you use the theming approach to customize the components, you'll still be able to override them using the `sx` prop as it has a higher CSS specificity, even if you're using the experimental `sx` syntax within the theme.
+
+## Themed custom components
+
+You can use the same theme features with your own components. Give the component a unique name, pass that name to `useThemeProps`, and configure the root with Material UI's `styled` utility. The `name` must match the key used under `theme.components`.
+
+```jsx
+import * as React from 'react';
+import { styled, useThemeProps } from '@mui/material/styles';
+
+const StatRoot = styled('div', {
+  name: 'MuiStat',
+  slot: 'Root',
+  overridesResolver: (props, styles) => styles.root,
+})(({ theme }) => ({
+  ...theme.typography.h6,
+  padding: theme.spacing(2),
+  borderRadius: theme.shape.borderRadius,
+}));
+
+const Stat = React.forwardRef(function Stat(inProps, ref) {
+  const props = useThemeProps({ props: inProps, name: 'MuiStat' });
+  const { value, unit, ...other } = props;
+  const ownerState = { ...props, value, unit };
+
+  return (
+    <StatRoot ref={ref} ownerState={ownerState} {...other}>
+      {value} {unit}
+    </StatRoot>
+  );
+});
+```
+
+The root slot automatically applies `styleOverrides.root` and matching `variants` from the theme:
+
+```js
+const theme = createTheme({
+  components: {
+    MuiStat: {
+      defaultProps: {
+        unit: 'kg',
+      },
+      styleOverrides: {
+        root: {
+          color: 'darkslategray',
+        },
+      },
+      variants: [
+        {
+          props: { variant: 'outlined' },
+          style: {
+            border: '1px solid currentColor',
+          },
+        },
+      ],
+    },
+  },
+});
+```
